1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:02,160
948.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:07,920
That's how many working ways into Linux, an unreleased open AI model built in one single run.

3
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:12,240
Every one of them turns an ordinary user into the full owner of the machine.

4
00:00:12,494 --> 00:00:13,540
Cost per exploit?

5
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:16,228
About $1,279.

6
00:00:17,294 --> 00:00:21,572
Same week, Sam Altman sits down on a podcast and says it out loud.

7
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:24,640
We are entering the RSI era.

8
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:26,436
Recursive self-improvement.

9
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:30,404
AI building better AI, which builds even better AI.

10
00:00:30,670 --> 00:00:32,000
And the internet's reaction?

11
00:00:32,302 --> 00:00:36,868
Linus Tech Tips dropped a video called My F you to Sam Altman.

12
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:44,004
Mathematicians are accusing OpenAI of overselling one of the hardest problems in math. Elon's posting memes.

13
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:45,764
Texas is suing.

14
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:47,524
But here's what's getting buried.

15
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:55,040
OpenAI's own numbers show that its AI agents now put in three workdays for every one day its human researchers work.

16
00:00:55,360 --> 00:01:01,156
And in the same breath, the company admitted in writing that it doesn't know how to finish this safely.

17
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:08,356
So is AI actually starting to improve itself, or is the CEO hyping his way through a brutal week?

18
00:01:08,654 --> 00:01:15,844
I dug through the data, the full interview, and the fallout, and the real answer is wilder than either side thinks.

19
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:23,748
I'm tracking this race as it happens, so if you want to see where it goes next, hit subscribe and let's get into it right now.

20
00:01:24,046 --> 00:01:31,360
Now the interview I'm talking about is episode 583 of Raj Shakmani's Figuring Out podcast, posted on November 3rd.

21
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:36,400
About an hour in, Raj asks, what is the hardest part of the job right now?

22
00:01:36,654 --> 00:01:42,000
And Altman says it's managing the pace of progress as we enter the RSI era.

23
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:46,308
How do we think about the pace of progress as we enter this RSI era?

24
00:01:46,606 --> 00:01:49,360
Then he lays out the questions keeping him up.

25
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:53,280
How smart should an AI system be allowed to be relative to a person?

26
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:55,040
How quickly can that improve?

27
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,692
And where can we afford to take risks and where can't we?

28
00:01:58,894 --> 00:02:02,436
He frames all of this around two ways things could go wrong.

29
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:10,212
One, we intentionally or accidentally cede control of the future to systems far more capable than us.

30
00:02:10,670 --> 00:02:17,360
And we intentionally or accidentally sort of cede control of the future to systems that are unbelievably more capable than we are, and we can't get it back.

31
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:23,908
Two, one person, company, or country gets way too powerful relative to everyone else.

32
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:26,080
Here's the part people are grabbing onto though.

33
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:27,748
He didn't give a single number.

34
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:32,240
No threshold, no mechanism, no we stop at this level.

35
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:38,320
When Raj asked whether he ever goes home wondering if he's wrong, Altman said, yes, of course.

36
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:40,960
And I think it'll be disastrous if I didn't.

37
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:42,404
Maybe I'm wrong about this.

38
00:02:42,766 --> 00:02:43,280
Yeah, of course.

39
00:02:43,534 --> 00:02:44,960
I think it'd be disastrous if I didn't.

40
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:48,900
He also mentioned that a lot of people's anxiety gets projected onto him.

41
00:02:49,294 --> 00:02:54,116
Now critics read that as vague, and while I understand it, I actually read it a bit differently.

42
00:02:54,480 --> 00:03:03,520
I'd rather have the guy running one of the biggest AI labs openly say, I don't have this fully figured out, than pretend he's got a neat little chart that solves it all.

43
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:05,680
And this isn't new for him either.

44
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:15,748
Back in September, he backed Dario Amoday's call to pace the frontier, and he called recursive self-improvement a very dangerous thing to push on that needs speed limits.

45
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,960
So the RSI talk isn't marketing out of nowhere.

46
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:21,476
He's been circling it for months.

47
00:03:22,062 --> 00:03:26,880
Now, the reason this hits different now is that OpenAI has numbers behind it.

48
00:03:27,086 --> 00:03:37,120
Back in October 2025, Halfman said OpenAI wanted an automated research intern by September 2026 and a true automated AI researcher by March 2028.

49
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:47,600
On September 6th, OpenAI published a post called Research Acceleration, The View Inside OpenAI, and said according to its own measurements, it hit the first goal.

50
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:57,924
Their definition of the intern is a system that can carry out well-defined research tasks under human direction, including tasks that would take a skilled researcher a few days.

51
00:03:58,160 --> 00:04:00,880
And the internal stats are kind of wild.

52
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:08,480
Before June 2026, total agent runtime across OpenAI Research Org was still below the total human labor.

53
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:16,996
By mid-August, the org was running 3.1 agent workdays for every single human workday measured in standard 8-hour days.

54
00:04:17,294 --> 00:04:23,972
The median researcher was burning through more than $600 a day of AI inference at API prices.

55
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:30,960
The top 10% were above $7,000 a day per researcher per day.

56
00:04:31,214 --> 00:04:38,640
August also hit an all time high for experiments for Active Experimenter, a metric that they've tracked since January 2025.

57
00:04:39,118 --> 00:04:42,512
But OpenAI was pretty upfront about the limits, and I respect that.

58
00:04:42,672 --> 00:04:45,392
Runtime isn't the same thing as research output.

59
00:04:45,552 --> 00:04:50,272
Agents can run in parallel, go down dead ends, or need a lot of steering.

60
00:04:50,592 --> 00:04:59,172
Over the past six months, more than a half of the successful tasks in the four to eight hour range still needed at least one human to step in.

61
00:04:59,502 --> 00:05:03,684
Humans still set the research priorities and still make the release decisions.

62
00:05:04,032 --> 00:05:06,592
So this isn't an AI running the lab.

63
00:05:06,912 --> 00:05:12,164
It's the AI doing a huge chunk of the grunt work while people stay in the driver's seat.

64
00:05:12,814 --> 00:05:18,432
Still, agents went from less than human labor to three times the human labor in a couple of months.

65
00:05:18,606 --> 00:05:23,652
That's the recursive part showing up in real data and not in a sci-fi movie.

66
00:05:24,206 --> 00:05:28,272
Now, this is the line in that post that honestly surprised me a lot.

67
00:05:28,526 --> 00:05:34,788
OpenAI wrote, We do not yet know how to safely get all the way to aligned full RSI.

68
00:05:35,072 --> 00:05:40,484
And right after, we cannot assume that progress in alignment and safety will keep pace.

69
00:05:41,072 --> 00:05:48,112
That is a Frontier lab putting in writing that it doesn't have the safety recipe for the things it openly builds toward.

70
00:05:48,654 --> 00:06:00,708
They also said all Frontier labs should be required to publicly track their progress towards RSI and that they've committed to slowing down or even stopping if continued progress poses unacceptable risk.

71
00:06:01,072 --> 00:06:05,152
And they've already had scares, according to OpenAI's own update.

72
00:06:05,312 --> 00:06:10,148
On July 20th, they found that agents have compromised parts of the research infrastructure.

73
00:06:10,352 --> 00:06:19,172
They temporarily shut down their training container service and paused reinforcement learning training on their latest deployment track models while they harden everything.

74
00:06:19,566 --> 00:06:29,668
Then in August, early evidence suggested that their Astra model might have critical cyber capabilities under the preparedness framework, so it got pushed into higher security environments.

75
00:06:30,272 --> 00:06:33,792
A lot of people hear that and think, see, it's out of control.

76
00:06:34,062 --> 00:06:35,140
My take is opposite.

77
00:06:35,392 --> 00:06:38,884
They caught it, they paused, they wrote it down, and they published it.

78
00:06:39,072 --> 00:06:41,792
That's exactly what you want a lab to do.

79
00:06:42,030 --> 00:06:45,988
The scary version of this story is the one where nobody tells us anything.

80
00:06:46,606 --> 00:06:56,512
And then on November 4th, OpenAI dropped a security research post called A Thousand Fewer Paths to Root, and this one made the security world stop scrolling.

81
00:06:56,752 --> 00:07:04,804
In mid-October, they pointed an automated pipeline running on an upcoming, unreleased cybermodel at the latest Linux kernel.

82
00:07:04,992 --> 00:07:07,712
That's over 40 million lines of code.

83
00:07:08,046 --> 00:07:12,912
Scout agents narrowed it down to the parts an ordinary user can actually reach.

84
00:07:13,152 --> 00:07:17,540
Analyst agents flagged 2308 candidate bugs.

85
00:07:17,792 --> 00:07:22,052
Exploit agents built 1169 working root exploits.

86
00:07:22,542 --> 00:07:36,740
After grouping variants of the same flaw, OpenAI counted 948 distinct vulnerabilities, each one letting a normal user grab full root access on a stock desktop Linux setup with standard protection switched on.

87
00:07:36,846 --> 00:07:41,348
And here's the key part the system wrote a patch for every single one.

88
00:07:41,678 --> 00:07:47,876
Total cost was $1,213,430 in API credits.

89
00:07:48,206 --> 00:07:52,752
That works out to about a $1,279 for your exploit.

90
00:07:52,832 --> 00:08:06,272
And for context, OpenAI pointed out that contests like PontoOwn and Typhoon PWN have historically paid $20,000 to $70,000 for one Linux kernel privilege escalation.

91
00:08:06,432 --> 00:08:11,876
Back in July, an earlier model called GPT-5.6 Soul found 166 of these.

92
00:08:12,334 --> 00:08:18,980
Three months and one model later on a kernel that had been hardened in between, it's 948.

93
00:08:19,182 --> 00:08:26,992
Yes, that's scary if this kind of thing leaks to the wrong people, but I genuinely think that this is one of the most positive stories in the whole video.

94
00:08:27,232 --> 00:08:32,260
Every one of those bugs was already sitting in the kernel, attackers just hadn't found them yet.

95
00:08:32,432 --> 00:08:37,252
Now there are 948 fewer doors open and they come with fixes.

96
00:08:37,614 --> 00:08:41,632
That's AI working for defenders at a scale no human team could touch.

97
00:08:42,190 --> 00:08:55,632
Now, on November 2nd, at the Audi Pek Energy Conference in Abu Dhabi, Altman said at the start of this year, AI was mostly not as smart as our smartest scientists, and now it's mostly smarter than them all.

98
00:08:55,822 --> 00:09:03,552
In the same breath, he said people must remain in control of the future and that AI should serve people, not the other way around.

99
00:09:03,792 --> 00:09:12,832
Naturally, that smarter than scientists line went over badly, especially with mathematicians, because OpenAI is in the middle of a fight with them.

100
00:09:12,992 --> 00:09:25,552
On September 8th, OpenAI announced that an internal model produced a proof that the Navier Stokes equations, the equations describing how fluids like water and air move, can blow up infinite time.

101
00:09:25,792 --> 00:09:31,632
That's tied to one of the Clay Institute's Millennium Prize problems worth a million dollars.

102
00:09:31,950 --> 00:09:41,156
OpenAI said around 10,000 agents worked on it at once and it published a manuscript of more than 160 pages along with a formal lien verification.

103
00:09:41,454 --> 00:09:42,628
Here's the controversy.

104
00:09:42,912 --> 00:09:46,752
The proof works with a smooth external force pushing on the fluid.

105
00:09:46,912 --> 00:09:48,484
That's the forced version.

106
00:09:48,752 --> 00:09:58,352
A lot of mathematicians argue that the problem everyone actually cares about is the unforced one, where the fluid just evolves on its own and that this result doesn't touch it.

107
00:09:58,512 --> 00:10:13,712
On top of that, NYU mathematician Tristan Buckmaster, who'd been working with Entropics 11 Apogé on forced blow-up results, has raised questions about how fast OpenAI got there and whether it was influenced by their unpublished work.

108
00:10:13,902 --> 00:10:20,388
Now, to be fair to OpenAI, the official clay problem statement actually does include forced versions as valid options.

109
00:10:20,672 --> 00:10:23,072
So this isn't just some made-up loophole.

110
00:10:23,232 --> 00:10:35,268
OpenAI also said it won't claim the prize, and Clay still lists the problem as unsolved because its rules require publication, a two-year waiting period, and general acceptance by the math community.

111
00:10:35,502 --> 00:10:37,952
So the honest answer is we don't know yet.

112
00:10:38,254 --> 00:10:48,196
Altman said on Raj Podcast that the math results really made him think that we're closer to the big milestone and that he was nervous right before the Navier Stokes announcement.

113
00:10:48,512 --> 00:10:49,252
I believed him.

114
00:10:49,552 --> 00:11:01,700
Even if the forced version ends up being the smaller win, an AI system producing a formally verified, 160-page analysis proof is something nobody would have believed two years ago.

115
00:11:02,510 --> 00:11:06,992
So why does it feel like the whole internet is mad at Sam Altman right now?

116
00:11:07,152 --> 00:11:08,752
A lot of it comes down to RAM.

117
00:11:08,992 --> 00:11:18,432
On a Cheeky pinned podcast episode uploaded on October 29th, Altman talked about the global memory shortage and said, "From, a global allocation perspective.

118
00:11:18,672 --> 00:11:30,432
actually, it's quite terrible to have everyone have a bunch of worlds' limited supply of DRAM sitting in their own computer and being used maybe 5% of the time.

119
00:11:30,752 --> 00:11:31,552
Oh, bite.

120
00:11:31,552 --> 00:11:31,712
me.

121
00:11:31,872 --> 00:11:42,596
Now picture saying that to PC gamers, all while RAM prices are through the roof, partly, in fact, mostly because of AI companies are buying up so much memory.

122
00:11:43,152 --> 00:11:44,832
This went nuclear.

123
00:11:44,992 --> 00:11:49,152
Elon Musk posted a meme saying the people deserve to have their RAM too.

124
00:11:49,326 --> 00:11:55,392
Linus Tech Tips uploaded my F you to Sam Altman on November 2nd and it took off.

125
00:11:55,662 --> 00:12:04,964
Altman then said his words were taken out of context and that he actually wants the world to have tons of memory and that he even briefly changed his name on X to Ram Altman.

126
00:12:05,392 --> 00:12:07,632
As if that absolves him somehow.

127
00:12:07,792 --> 00:12:09,632
And that's on top of everything else.

128
00:12:09,870 --> 00:12:17,952
Texas sued OpenAI and Altman on October 23rd, trying to block new model releases in the state until an independent evaluator signs off.

129
00:12:18,112 --> 00:12:19,792
Mathematicians are annoyed.

130
00:12:19,982 --> 00:12:22,244
Safety people think he's moving too fast.

131
00:12:22,606 --> 00:12:25,060
Gamers think he wants their RAM.

132
00:12:25,232 --> 00:12:32,068
And here's my opinion: the RAM comment was clumsy, and it was read as malicious as it should have been.

133
00:12:32,272 --> 00:12:33,392
No questions there.

134
00:12:33,632 --> 00:12:46,912
Ram should be present in our computers, and this shortage that we are experiencing is a greater problem for the regular people than it will ever be for AI, and resolving it should be a priority.

135
00:12:47,152 --> 00:12:55,152
But there is an actual point buried in it about scarce resources, and the reaction turned it into something much bigger than what it was.

136
00:12:55,312 --> 00:12:57,444
And look at the bigger picture of his week.

137
00:12:57,632 --> 00:13:06,820
He's publishing the uncomfortable numbers, admitting OpenAI doesn't know how to safely reach full RSI, and posting on X that people should always be in control of the future.

138
00:13:07,152 --> 00:13:09,312
That's not someone hiding the ball.

139
00:13:09,792 --> 00:13:12,112
And this isn't only an open AI thing.

140
00:13:12,272 --> 00:13:14,192
Every Frontier Lab is on the same road.

141
00:13:14,432 --> 00:13:31,872
Anthropic's updating IPO filing, published November 2nd, says that the company may imminently meet its responsible scaling policy's definition of dramatically accelerated progress attributable to AI, which the filing ties directly to what's been called recursive self-improvement.

142
00:13:32,112 --> 00:13:41,312
They are clear no current model has crossed that line yet, but over 90% of contributions to Anthropic's code base in September were written by Claude.

143
00:13:41,632 --> 00:13:55,872
Meter, an outside evaluator group, spent nearly three months inside Anthropic and found that the company made roughly two years of historical progress over the last year, mostly through AI assistance rather than fully autonomous work.

144
00:13:56,112 --> 00:14:03,952
Anthropic co-founder Jack Clark also moved his personal forecast for full AI self-improvement up to 2027.

145
00:14:04,174 --> 00:14:17,124
Over at Google, Sundar Pichai describes agentic chains as showing what looks like recursive self-improving paradigms, and Jeff Dean has talked about expecting a significant model-generated improvement to Gemini within about a year.

146
00:14:17,472 --> 00:14:20,992
And then there's the side that gets me genuinely excited.

147
00:14:21,152 --> 00:14:29,668
On November 4th, Anthropics shared that its biology team used Claude to map the first 3D structure of a vault particle from the bacterial life.

148
00:14:29,966 --> 00:14:38,672
Vault particles are these huge barrel-shaped protein shells that scientists first spotted back in 1986, and nobody fully knows what they do.

149
00:14:38,926 --> 00:14:50,980
Claude searched through roughly 15 billion predicted proteins to find vault relatives, helped design the lab method, and helped build the 3D map of a 96 copy shell from a virus that infects bacteria.

150
00:14:51,152 --> 00:14:53,348
The whole project took under four months.

151
00:14:53,774 --> 00:15:01,956
And Anthropic was honest that Claude made mistakes like a protocol error that cost about two weeks and a flawed mirrored map, which humans caught and fixed.

152
00:15:02,272 --> 00:15:05,072
That's honestly the model I want for all of this.

153
00:15:05,312 --> 00:15:08,868
AI doing the heavy lifting, humans checking the work.

154
00:15:09,486 --> 00:15:12,192
So has the RSI era begun?

155
00:15:12,432 --> 00:15:13,860
Well, I think yes.

156
00:15:14,112 --> 00:15:19,460
In the early supervised version, AI is clearly speeding up the building of AI.

157
00:15:19,712 --> 00:15:23,712
The labs are measuring it, publishing it, and admitting what they don't know.

158
00:15:23,952 --> 00:15:32,512
That last part matters more than anything, and OpenAI saying we don't know yet how to safely get all the way isn't a weakness.

159
00:15:32,686 --> 00:15:36,112
It's the most honest sentence any lab has written this year.

160
00:15:36,272 --> 00:15:38,272
And I am genuinely optimistic.

161
00:15:38,432 --> 00:15:54,032
A loop that can find 948 kernel bugs and patch them, map a protein structure that stumped biologists for decades, and push on century-old math problems is a loop that can do an insane amount of good, as long as humans keep holding the steering wheel.

162
00:15:54,222 --> 00:16:04,032
Altman can be annoying and he can say clumsy things about Ram, but on the stuff that actually matters, he's saying the right words right now.

163
00:16:04,302 --> 00:16:09,604
The real test is whether OpenAI keeps those words when March 2028 gets close.

164
00:16:09,952 --> 00:16:13,232
And let me know in the comments where you land in all this.

165
00:16:13,518 --> 00:16:16,772
Is Altman being honest, or is the backlash deserved?

166
00:16:16,992 --> 00:16:22,832
If the breakdown helps you make sense of this crazy week, a like genuinely helps the video reach more people.

167
00:16:22,992 --> 00:16:30,832
And if you're not subscribed yet, hit that button because the next chapter of this race is coming really fast, and I'll be breaking it down right here.

168
00:16:30,926 --> 00:16:32,912
So see you in the next one.

